            <ArticleTitle>Splenic rupture due to metastatic disease: a rare complication.</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">bcr-2017-221336</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1136/bcr-2017-221336</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>We report a case of splenic rupture in an 83-year-old woman presenting with symptoms concerning for upper gastrointestinal malignancy. CT imaging revealed marked gastric thickening and a 33×33 mm splenic metastasis, with subsequent gastroscopy demonstrating a large lesion at the gastro-oesophageal junction. While awaiting further investigation, the patient developed acute onset left upper quadrant pain, peritonism and hypovolaemic shock. Urgent CT imaging demonstrated a ruptured spleen with extensive haemoperitoneum, necessitating emergency splenectomy.</AbstractText>
